('CIT_CHRR_NEW_JERUSALEM_AUGUST','ENT_CHRR_NEW_JERUSALEM','SRC_AUGUSTINE_CITY_OF_GOD','Augustine, City of God','Augustine, City of God','Their blood have they shed like water round about Jerusalem; and there was none to bury them." But this was said rather to exhibit the cruelty of those who did these things, than the misery of those who suffered them.','Marcus Dods',1871,'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45304',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'English translation located by name within the work; verify book.chapter locus.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_ROM_CUNINA_AUGUST','ENT_ROM_CUNINA','SRC_AUGUSTINE_CITY_OF_GOD','Augustine, City of God','Augustine, City of God','Now, surely of this work, which is so excellent and so very full of the highest dignity, they dare not ascribe any part to the goddess Cloacina; or to Volupia, who has her appellation from voluptuousness; or to Libentina, who has her name from lust; or to Vaticanus, who presides over the screaming of infants; or to Cunina, who rules over their cradles.','Marcus Dods',1871,'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45304',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'English translation located by name within the work; verify book.chapter locus.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_ROM_PILUMNUS_AUGUST','ENT_ROM_PILUMNUS','SRC_AUGUSTINE_CITY_OF_GOD','Augustine, City of God','Augustine, City of God','Now from these three things three gods have been named: Intercidona, from the cut made by the hatchet; Pilumnus, from the pestle; Diverra, from the besom;--by which guardian gods the woman who has been delivered is preserved against the power of the god Silvanus.','Marcus Dods',1871,'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45304',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'English translation located by name within the work; verify book.chapter locus.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_ROM_SEGETIA_AUGUST','ENT_ROM_SEGETIA','SRC_AUGUSTINE_CITY_OF_GOD','Augustine, City of God','Augustine, City of God','Nor could they even find one Segetia so competent, that they could commend to her care all their corn crops at once; but so long as their seed-corn was still under the ground, they would have the goddess Seia set over it; then, whenever it was above ground and formed straw, they set over it the goddess Segetia; and when the grain was collected and stored, they set over it the goddess Tutilina, that it might be kept safe.','Marcus Dods',1871,'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45304',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'English translation located by name within the work; verify book.chapter locus.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1),
('CIT_ROM_TUTILINA_AUGUST','ENT_ROM_TUTILINA','SRC_AUGUSTINE_CITY_OF_GOD','Augustine, City of God','Augustine, City of God','Nor could they even find one Segetia so competent, that they could commend to her care all their corn crops at once; but so long as their seed-corn was still under the ground, they would have the goddess Seia set over it; then, whenever it was above ground and formed straw, they set over it the goddess Segetia; and when the grain was collected and stored, they set over it the goddess Tutilina, that it might be kept safe.','Marcus Dods',1871,'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45304',NULL,'primary-verbatim',NULL,true,'English translation located by name within the work; verify book.chapter locus.',DATE '2026-06-18','name-anchored (note-keyword scored) + substring gate; locus per attestation',1)
